1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,834
A first-level model can contain several explanatory variables, but a scientific result usually begins with a narrower question.

2
00:00:07,834 --> 00:00:13,491
Was the response during left-button trials greater than the response during right-button trials?

3
00:00:13,491 --> 00:00:17,843
Was one condition positively related to the measured BOLD signal?

4
00:00:17,843 --> 00:00:23,501
A contrast turns a question like that into a precise set of weights.

5
00:00:23,501 --> 00:00:39,604
In this lesson, we will connect those weights to the parameter estimates in the general linear model, interpret baseline and difference contrasts, and identify the quality-control checks that prevent a correct calculation from answering the wrong question.

6
00:00:40,454 --> 00:00:44,489
At each voxel, FEAT fits the general linear model.

7
00:00:44,489 --> 00:00:53,006
The design matrix contains the explanatory variables, or EVs, and beta contains one estimated coefficient for each model column.

8
00:00:53,006 --> 00:01:01,076
Those coefficients describe how strongly each predictor contributes to the observed signal after accounting for the other predictors.

9
00:01:01,076 --> 00:01:07,800
A contrast does not refit the model and does not change the original beta estimates.

10
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:11,835
Instead, it defines a linear combination of selected estimates.

11
00:01:11,835 --> 00:01:16,318
In compact notation, the contrast estimate is c transpose beta.

12
00:01:16,318 --> 00:01:20,801
The vector c contains the weights that encode the hypothesis.

13
00:01:21,651 --> 00:01:25,759
Suppose the original EV order is Left followed by Right.

14
00:01:25,759 --> 00:01:29,045
The contrast one, zero selects the Left coefficient.

15
00:01:29,045 --> 00:01:38,904
In the usual FEAT task model, it tests a positive Left effect relative to the implicit baseline represented by the rest of the model.

16
00:01:38,904 --> 00:01:41,368
Zero, one selects the Right coefficient.

17
00:01:41,368 --> 00:01:45,066
These are not direct comparisons between Left and Right.

18
00:01:45,066 --> 00:01:49,995
They ask whether each selected estimate is positive in its modeled context.

19
00:01:49,995 --> 00:01:55,335
A contrast using one, one tests the positive sum of the two coefficients.

20
00:01:55,335 --> 00:01:59,032
Using one half, one half gives their numerical average.

21
00:01:59,032 --> 00:02:08,891
Those scaled contrasts have the same t or z statistic, but their cope values are on different scales, so name and report them carefully.

22
00:02:09,741 --> 00:02:14,033
To compare Left directly with Right, use one, negative one.

23
00:02:14,033 --> 00:02:17,037
This computes beta Left minus beta Right.

24
00:02:17,037 --> 00:02:26,050
The null hypothesis is that the weighted difference equals zero, and the positive direction tests whether Left is greater than Right.

25
00:02:26,050 --> 00:02:31,629
Reversing the signs gives negative one, one, which tests Right greater than Left.

26
00:02:31,629 --> 00:02:34,634
A negative weight does not mean deactivation.

27
00:02:34,634 --> 00:02:39,784
It means that the corresponding coefficient is subtracted in this particular hypothesis.

28
00:02:39,784 --> 00:02:49,226
Direction matters: Left greater than Right and Right greater than Left are related, but they are not interchangeable labels for one contrast.

29
00:02:50,076 --> 00:02:56,188
Contrast numbers only have meaning when they are aligned with the correct design-matrix columns.

30
00:02:56,188 --> 00:03:08,411
If the actual order is Right then Left, the weights one, negative one test Right greater than Left, even if the contrast is named Left greater than Right.

31
00:03:08,411 --> 00:03:18,451
Before running FEAT, write the EV order, read each contrast as a sentence, and point from every weight to its displayed column name.

32
00:03:18,451 --> 00:03:20,634
Then inspect the design preview.

33
00:03:20,634 --> 00:03:27,182
Empty columns, swapped timing files, duplicated predictors, or unexpected derivatives can all change the interpretation.

34
00:03:27,182 --> 00:03:31,111
A plausible activation map cannot repair a mislabeled hypothesis.

35
00:03:31,961 --> 00:03:36,652
In FEAT, open Full model setup and then Contrasts and F-tests.

36
00:03:36,652 --> 00:03:41,344
Give each contrast a scientific name rather than a generic number.

37
00:03:41,344 --> 00:03:44,757
Enter the weights in the displayed EV order.

38
00:03:44,757 --> 00:03:55,420
For the source lab, the model contains Left and Right EVs, followed by contrasts for each condition, both directional differences, and a combined positive effect.

39
00:03:55,420 --> 00:04:01,391
The screenshot here is evidence from the real teaching workflow, not a generated interface.

40
00:04:01,391 --> 00:04:10,775
Before selecting Done, compare every row with the approved analysis plan and save the design file so the model specification remains reproducible.

41
00:04:11,625 --> 00:04:15,460
For each contrast, FEAT writes several related outputs.

42
00:04:15,460 --> 00:04:23,609
The cope is the contrast of parameter estimates: the weighted effect in the units of the model.

43
00:04:23,609 --> 00:04:27,444
The varcope describes uncertainty in that contrast estimate.

44
00:04:27,444 --> 00:04:37,990
Dividing the cope by its standard error produces a t statistic, which FEAT converts to a z statistic for inference and reporting.

45
00:04:37,990 --> 00:04:43,263
A z-statistic map is therefore standardized evidence, not an effect-size map.

46
00:04:43,263 --> 00:04:47,577
Higher-level analyses use the cope together with its varcope.

47
00:04:47,577 --> 00:04:58,602
Thresholded z-statistic images help visualize inferential results, but interpretation should begin only after checking preprocessing, registration, the design matrix, and the contrast definition.

48
00:04:59,452 --> 00:05:02,733
Before you run FEAT, complete five checks.

49
00:05:02,733 --> 00:05:05,545
First, list EVs in matrix order.

50
00:05:05,545 --> 00:05:09,294
Second, state the scientific question for every contrast.

51
00:05:09,294 --> 00:05:16,324
Third, translate the sentence into weights and then translate the weights back into a sentence.

52
00:05:16,324 --> 00:05:20,541
Fourth, inspect the design matrix and contrast arrows visually.

53
00:05:20,541 --> 00:05:27,571
Fifth, distinguish the effect estimate, its uncertainty, and the standardized statistic you will later report.

54
00:05:27,571 --> 00:05:35,538
If the design, weights, and scientific language tell the same story, the contrast is ready to run.

55
00:05:35,538 --> 00:05:44,443
Next, the hands-on walkthrough will configure these contrasts in a live Neurodesk session and inspect the corresponding FEAT outputs.
